 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to requiring the commissioner\nof education to make recommendations to the board of regents relating to the\nadoption of instruction in information relating to blood, bone marrow and\norgan and tissue donations in senior high schools",
    "basePrintNo": "S8458",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8458-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §305, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8458\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nrequiring the commissioner of education to make recommendations to the\nboard of regents relating to the adoption of instruction in information\nrelating to blood, bone marrow and organ and tissue donations in senior\nhigh schools\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill requires the commissioner of education to make recommendations\nto the board of regents relating to the adoption of instruction in\ninformation relating to blood, bone marrow and organ and tissue\ndonations.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 305 of the education law is amended by adding a new subdivision\n55 that would require the State Education Department to consider if\ninstruction in organ and tissue, blood and bone marrow donations in high\nschools can be beneficial.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York has the unfortunate distinction of being 50th in the nation in\norgan and tissue donation. For a myriad number of reasons, the number of\ndonors in New York is not nearly enough to meet the needs of potential\nrecipients. There are more than 10,000 individuals currently waiting for\nan organ and tissue donation in the State, and unfortunately due to the\nsevere shortage of donors, an individual passes away every fifteen hours\nbecause they could not get a critically needed donation.\n\nIt is not just organ and tissue donation that New York must improve on.\nBlood donations and bone marrow donations are two other important types\nof donations that can help save and improve the lives of others.\n\nIn New York, the current age of consent to register as a bone marrow and\norgan and tissue donor is 18. The age of consent to donate blood is 17\n(or 16 with parental consent). Therefore, it is critical our students be\nfully informed of the numerous lifesaving benefits of each of these\ntypes of donations before they reach the age of consent so they can make\nan informed decision on whether to register as a donor. Moreover,\nstudents that are fully informed can have constructive dialogues with\ntheir families and friends about the benefits of being a donor.\n\nIt goes without saying that being a donor is one of the easiest ways to\nhave a profound impact on the lives of others. One organ donor has the\nability to save up to 8 lives, and impact up to 50 more. Similarly, one\npint of blood from one donor has the ability save up to 3 lives.\n\nNew York must do better to raise the number of donors in the state.  One\nof the most effective ways to do so is to keep asking and keep inform-\ning. This bill will require all students to be informed about organ and\ntissue, bone marrow and blood donations and will hopefully result in a\ngreater number of people becoming donors and saving lives.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
